  "id": "a52990c78f89",
  "title": "Oxford Electric Bell",
  "originalTitle": "Oxford Electric Bell",
  "source": "Hacker News",
  "category": "Tech",
  "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxford_Electric_Bell",
  "publishedAt": "2026-08-14T05:46:05.000Z",
  "content": "<div id=\"readability-page-1\" class=\"page\"><div> <p>From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia</p> </div><div lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\" data-mw-parsoid-version=\"0.24.0.0-alpha18\" data-mw-html-version=\"2.8.0\" id=\"mw-content-text\"><section data-mw-section-id=\"0\" id=\"mwAQ\"><span about=\"#mwt1\"></span> <p id=\"mwDg\">The <b id=\"mwDw\">Oxford Electric Bell</b> or <b id=\"mwEA\">Clarendon Dry Pile</b> is an <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Experiment\" title=\"Experiment\" id=\"mwEQ\">experimental</a> <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric_bell\" title=\"Electric bell\" id=\"mwEg\">electric bell</a>, in particular a type of bell that uses the electrostatic clock principle, that was set up in 1840 and which has run nearly continuously ever since. It was one of the first pieces purchased for a collection of apparatus by clergyman and physicist Robert Walker.<sup about=\"#mwt5\" id=\"cite_ref-1\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw=\"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-1&quot;}}\"><a href=\"#cite_note-1\" id=\"mwEw\"><span id=\"mwFA\"><span id=\"mwFQ\">[</span>1<span id=\"mwFg\">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=\"#mwt8\" id=\"cite_ref-Oxford_2-0\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw=\"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Oxford&quot;},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-Oxford-2&quot;}}\"><a href=\"#cite_note-Oxford-2\" id=\"mwFw\"><span id=\"mwGA\"><span id=\"mwGQ\">[</span>2<span id=\"mwGg\">]</span></span></a></sup> It is located in a corridor adjacent to the foyer of the <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clarendon_Laboratory\" title=\"Clarendon Laboratory\" id=\"mwGw\">Clarendon Laboratory</a> at the <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Oxford\" title=\"University of Oxford\" id=\"mwHA\">University of Oxford</a>, <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/England\" title=\"England\" id=\"mwHQ\">England</a>, and is still ringing every half second, albeit inaudibly due to being behind two layers of glass.</p>\n\n<meta property=\"mw:PageProp/toc\" id=\"mwHg\" data-mw=\"{&quot;autoGenerated&quot;:true}\"></section><section data-mw-section-id=\"1\" id=\"mwHw\" aria-labelledby=\"Design\">\n<p id=\"mwIA\">The experiment consists of two <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brass\" title=\"Brass\" id=\"mwIQ\">brass</a> <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bell_(instrument)\" title=\"Bell (instrument)\" id=\"mwIg\">bells</a>, each positioned beneath a <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voltaic_pile\" title=\"Voltaic pile\" id=\"mwIw\">dry pile</a> (a form of <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battery_(electricity)\" title=\"Battery (electricity)\" id=\"mwJA\">battery</a>), the pair of piles connected in <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Series_circuit\" title=\"Series circuit\" id=\"mwJQ\">series</a>, giving the bells opposite <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric_charge\" title=\"Electric charge\" id=\"mwJg\">electric charges</a>. The clapper is a metal sphere approximately <span about=\"#mwt9\">4</span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\" about=\"#mwt9\">&nbsp;</span><span about=\"#mwt9\">mm (</span><span about=\"#mwt9\"><span>3</span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\">⁄</span><span>16</span></span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\" about=\"#mwt9\">&nbsp;</span><span about=\"#mwt9\">in)</span> in diameter suspended between the piles, which rings the bells alternately due to <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electrostatic_force\" title=\"Electrostatic force\" id=\"mwKA\">electrostatic forces</a>. When the clapper touches one bell, it is charged by that pile. It is then repelled from that bell due to having the same charge and attracted to the other bell, which has the opposite charge. The clapper then touches the other bell and the process reverses, leading to <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oscillation\" title=\"Oscillation\" id=\"mwKQ\">oscillation</a>. The use of electrostatic forces means that while high <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voltage\" title=\"Voltage\" id=\"mwKg\">voltage</a> is required to create motion, only a tiny amount of charge is carried from one bell to the other. As a result, the batteries drain very slowly, which is why the piles have been able to last since the apparatus was set up in 1840. Its oscillation frequency is 2 <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hertz\" title=\"Hertz\" id=\"mwKw\">hertz</a>, or every half second.<sup about=\"#mwt11\" id=\"cite_ref-3\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw=\"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-3&quot;}}\"><a href=\"#cite_note-3\" id=\"mwLA\"><span id=\"mwLQ\"><span id=\"mwLg\">[</span>3<span id=\"mwLw\">]</span></span></a></sup></p>\n\n<p id=\"mwMA\">The exact composition of the dry piles is unknown, but it is known that they have been coated with molten <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulfur\" title=\"Sulfur\" id=\"mwMQ\">sulfur</a> for insulation and it is thought that they may be <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zamboni_pile\" title=\"Zamboni pile\" id=\"mwMg\">Zamboni piles</a>.<sup about=\"#mwt12\" id=\"cite_ref-Oxford_2-1\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw=\"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Oxford&quot;}}\"><a href=\"#cite_note-Oxford-2\" id=\"mwMw\"><span id=\"mwNA\"><span id=\"mwNQ\">[</span>2<span id=\"mwNg\">]</span></span></a></sup></p>\n\n<p id=\"mwNw\">At one point this sort of device played an important role in distinguishing between two different theories of electrical action: the theory of <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Contact_tension\" title=\"Contact tension\" id=\"mwOA\">contact tension</a> and the theory of <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electrochemistry\" title=\"Electrochemistry\" id=\"mwOQ\">chemical action</a>.<sup about=\"#mwt15\" id=\"cite_ref-4\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw=\"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-4&quot;}}\"><a href=\"#cite_note-4\" id=\"mwOg\"><span id=\"mwOw\"><span id=\"mwPA\">[</span>4<span id=\"mwPQ\">]</span></span></a></sup></p>\n\n<p id=\"mwPg\">The Oxford Electric Bell does not demonstrate <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perpetual_motion\" title=\"Perpetual motion\" id=\"mwPw\">perpetual motion</a>. The bell will eventually stop when the dry piles have distributed their charges equally if the clapper does not wear out first.<sup about=\"#mwt18\" id=\"cite_ref-5\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw=\"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-5&quot;}}\"><a href=\"#cite_note-5\" id=\"mwQA\"><span id=\"mwQQ\"><span id=\"mwQg\">[</span>5<span id=\"mwQw\">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=\"#mwt20\" id=\"cite_ref-6\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw=\"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-6&quot;}}\"><a href=\"#cite_note-6\" id=\"mwRA\"><span id=\"mwRQ\"><span id=\"mwRg\">[</span>6<span id=\"mwRw\">]</span></span></a></sup> The Bell has produced approximately 10 billion rings since 1840 and holds the Guinness World Record as \"the world's most durable battery [delivering] ceaseless <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink/Interwiki\" href=\"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tintinnabulation\" title=\"wikt:tintinnabulation\" id=\"mwSA\">tintinnabulation</a>\".<sup about=\"#mwt21\" id=\"cite_ref-Oxford_2-2\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw=\"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Oxford&quot;}}\"><a href=\"#cite_note-Oxford-2\" id=\"mwSQ\"><span id=\"mwSg\"><span id=\"mwSw\">[</span>2<span id=\"mwTA\">]</span></span></a></sup></p>\n\n</section><section data-mw-section-id=\"2\" id=\"mwTQ\" aria-labelledby=\"Operation\">\n<p id=\"mwTg\">Apart from occasional short interruptions caused by high humidity, the bell has rung continuously since 1840.<sup about=\"#mwt24\" id=\"cite_ref-7\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw=\"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-7&quot;}}\"><a href=\"#cite_note-7\" id=\"mwTw\"><span id=\"mwUA\"><span id=\"mwUQ\">[</span>7<span id=\"mwUg\">]</span></span></a></sup> The bell may have been constructed in 1825.<sup about=\"#mwt25\" id=\"cite_ref-Oxford_2-3\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw=\"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Oxford&quot;}}\"><a href=\"#cite_note-Oxford-2\" id=\"mwUw\"><span id=\"mwVA\"><span id=\"mwVQ\">[</span>2<span id=\"mwVg\">]</span></span></a></sup></p>\n\n</section><section data-mw-section-id=\"3\" id=\"mwVw\" aria-labelledby=\"See_also\">\n<ul id=\"mwWA\"><li id=\"mwWQ\"><a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long-term_experiment\" title=\"Long-term experiment\" id=\"mwWg\">Long-term experiment</a></li>\n<li id=\"mwWw\"><a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franklin_bells\" title=\"Franklin bells\" id=\"mwXA\">Franklin bells</a></li>\n<li id=\"mwXQ\"><a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beverly_Clock\" title=\"Beverly Clock\" id=\"mwXg\">Beverly Clock</a> (1864)</li>\n<li id=\"mwXw\"><a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pitch_drop_experiment\" title=\"Pitch drop experiment\" id=\"mwYA\">Pitch drop experiment</a> (1927)</li>\n<li id=\"mwYQ\"><a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clock_of_the_Long_Now\" title=\"Clock of the Long Now\" id=\"mwYg\">Clock of the Long Now</a></li></ul>\n\n</section><section data-mw-section-id=\"4\" id=\"mwYw\" aria-labelledby=\"References\">\n<span about=\"#mwt26\" typeof=\"mw:Transclusion\" id=\"mwZA\" data-mw=\"{&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;template&quot;:{&quot;target&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;reflist&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:&quot;./Template:Reflist&quot;},&quot;params&quot;:{},&quot;i&quot;:0}}]}\"></span><div typeof=\"mw:Extension/references\" about=\"#mwt26\" id=\"mwZQ\" data-mw=\"{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;references&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{&quot;group&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;responsive&quot;:&quot;1&quot;},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;extsrc&quot;:&quot;&quot;}}\"><ol id=\"mwZw\"><li about=\"#cite_note-1\" id=\"cite_note-1\" data-mw-footnote-number=\"1\"><span id=\"mwaA\"><a href=\"#cite_ref-1\" rel=\"mw:referencedBy\" id=\"mwaQ\"><span id=\"mwag\">↑</span></a></span> <span id=\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-1\"><cite about=\"#mwt3\" id=\"mwbA\">\"Walker, Robert\". <i id=\"mwbQ\"><a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dictionary_of_National_Biography#Oxford_Dictionary_of_National_Biography\" title=\"Dictionary of National Biography\" id=\"mwbg\">Oxford Dictionary of National Biography</a></i> (online<span typeof=\"mw:Entity\" id=\"mwbw\">&nbsp;</span>ed.). Oxford University Press. <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doi_(identifier)\" title=\"Doi (identifier)\" id=\"mwcA\">doi</a>:<a rel=\"mw:ExtLink nofollow\" href=\"https://doi.org/10.1093%2Fref%3Aodnb%2F38098\" id=\"mwcQ\">10.1093/ref:odnb/38098</a>.</cite><span title=\"ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Abook&amp;rft.genre=bookitem&amp;rft.atitle=Walker%2C+Robert&amp;rft.btitle=Oxford+Dictionary+of+National+Biography&amp;rft.edition=online&amp;rft.pub=Oxford+University+Press&amp;rft_id=info%3Adoi%2F10.1093%2Fref%3Aodnb%2F38098&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3AOxford+Electric+Bell\" about=\"#mwt3\" id=\"mwcg\"></span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\" about=\"#mwt3\" id=\"mwcw\"> </span><span about=\"#mwt3\" id=\"mwdA\">(Subscription, <a rel=\"mw:ExtLink nofollow\" href=\"https://wikipedialibrary.wmflabs.org/partners/88/\" id=\"mwdQ\">Wikipedia Library</a> access or <a rel=\"mw:ExtLink nofollow\" href=\"https://www.oxforddnb.com/help/subscribe#public\" id=\"mwdg\">UK public library membership</a> required.)</span></span></li>\n<li about=\"#cite_note-Oxford-2\" id=\"cite_note-Oxford-2\" data-mw-footnote-number=\"2\"><span rel=\"mw:referencedBy\" id=\"mweQ\"><a href=\"#cite_ref-Oxford_2-0\" id=\"mweg\"><span id=\"mwew\">1</span></a> <a href=\"#cite_ref-Oxford_2-1\" id=\"mwfA\"><span id=\"mwfQ\">2</span></a> <a href=\"#cite_ref-Oxford_2-2\" id=\"mwfg\"><span id=\"mwfw\">3</span></a> <a href=\"#cite_ref-Oxford_2-3\" id=\"mwgA\"><span id=\"mwgQ\">4</span></a></span> <span id=\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Oxford-2\"><cite about=\"#mwt6\" id=\"mwgw\"><a rel=\"mw:ExtLink nofollow\" href=\"https://archwww.physics.ox.ac.uk/history/Exhibit1.html\" id=\"mwhA\">\"Exhibit 1 – The Clarendon Dry Pile\"</a>. <i id=\"mwhQ\">Department of Physics</i>. Oxford University<span id=\"mwhg\">. Retrieved <span id=\"mwhw\">30 January</span> 2021</span>.</cite><span title=\"ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;rft.genre=unknown&amp;rft.jtitle=Department+of+Physics&amp;rft.atitle=Exhibit+1+%E2%80%93+The+Clarendon+Dry+Pile&amp;rft_id=https%3A%2F%2Farchwww.physics.ox.ac.uk%2Fhistory%2FExhibit1.html&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3AOxford+Electric+Bell\" about=\"#mwt6\" id=\"mwiA\"></span></span></li>\n<li about=\"#cite_note-3\" id=\"cite_note-3\" data-mw-footnote-number=\"3\"><span id=\"mwiQ\"><a href=\"#cite_ref-3\" rel=\"mw:referencedBy\" id=\"mwig\"><span id=\"mwiw\">↑</span></a></span> <span id=\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-3\"><a rel=\"mw:ExtLink nofollow\" href=\"http://atlasobscura.com/place/oxford-electric-bell\" id=\"mwjA\">Oxford Electric Bell</a>, <i id=\"mwjQ\"><a rel=\"mw:ExtLink nofollow\" href=\"http://atlasobscura.com/\" id=\"mwjg\">Atlas Obscura</a></i>.</span></li>\n<li about=\"#cite_note-4\" id=\"cite_note-4\" data-mw-footnote-number=\"4\"><span id=\"mwjw\"><a href=\"#cite_ref-4\" rel=\"mw:referencedBy\" id=\"mwkA\"><span id=\"mwkQ\">↑</span></a></span> <span id=\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-4\"><cite id=\"CITEREFWillem_Hackmann\" about=\"#mwt13\">Willem Hackmann. <a rel=\"mw:ExtLink nofollow\" href=\"http://ppp.unipv.it/Collana/Pages/Libri/Saggi/Nuova%20Voltiana3_PDF/cap5/5new.pdf\" id=\"mwkw\">\"The Enigma of Volta's 'Contact Tension' and the Development of the 'Dry Pile'<span id=\"mwlA\"></span>\"</a> <span id=\"mwlQ\">(PDF)</span>. ppp.unipv.it<span id=\"mwlg\">. Retrieved <span id=\"mwlw\">2 March</span> 2018</span>.</cite><span title=\"ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Abook&amp;rft.genre=unknown&amp;rft.btitle=The+Enigma+of+Volta%27s+%27Contact+Tension%27+and+the+Development+of+the+%27Dry+Pile%27&amp;rft.pub=ppp.unipv.it&amp;rft.au=Willem+Hackmann&amp;rft_id=http%3A%2F%2Fppp.unipv.it%2FCollana%2FPages%2FLibri%2FSaggi%2FNuova%2520Voltiana3_PDF%2Fcap5%2F5new.pdf&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3AOxford+Electric+Bell\" about=\"#mwt13\" id=\"mwmA\"></span></span></li>\n<li about=\"#cite_note-5\" id=\"cite_note-5\" data-mw-footnote-number=\"5\"><span id=\"mwmQ\"><a href=\"#cite_ref-5\" rel=\"mw:referencedBy\" id=\"mwmg\"><span id=\"mwmw\">↑</span></a></span> <span id=\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-5\"><a rel=\"mw:ExtLink nofollow\" href=\"https://web.archive.org/web/20160303225723/http://thelongestlistofthelongeststuffatthelongestdomainnameatlonglast.com/long203.html\" about=\"#mwt16\" typeof=\"mw:Transclusion\" id=\"mwnA\" data-mw=\"{&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;template&quot;:{&quot;target&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;usurped&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:&quot;./Template:Usurped&quot;},&quot;params&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;[https://web.archive.org/web/20160303225723/http://thelongestlistofthelongeststuffatthelongestdomainnameatlonglast.com/long203.html The World's Longest Experiment]&quot;}},&quot;i&quot;:0}}]}\">The World's Longest Experiment</a>, <i id=\"mwnQ\"><a rel=\"mw:ExtLink nofollow\" href=\"https://web.archive.org/web/20050923185408/http://thelongestlistofthelongeststuffatthelongestdomainnameatlonglast.com/\" about=\"#mwt17\" typeof=\"mw:Transclusion\" id=\"mwng\" data-mw=\"{&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;template&quot;:{&quot;target&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;usurped&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:&quot;./Template:Usurped&quot;},&quot;params&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;[https://web.archive.org/web/20050923185408/http://thelongestlistofthelongeststuffatthelongestdomainnameatlonglast.com/ The Longest List of the Longest Stuff at the Longest Domain Name at Long Last]&quot;}},&quot;i&quot;:0}}]}\">The Longest List of the Longest Stuff at the Longest Domain Name at Long Last</a></i>.</span></li>\n<li about=\"#cite_note-6\" id=\"cite_note-6\" data-mw-footnote-number=\"6\"><span id=\"mwnw\"><a href=\"#cite_ref-6\" rel=\"mw:referencedBy\" id=\"mwoA\"><span id=\"mwoQ\">↑</span></a></span> <span id=\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-6\"><a rel=\"mw:ExtLink nofollow\" href=\"http://improbable.com/airchives/paperair/volume7/v7i3/long-run-7-3.html\" id=\"mwog\">The Latest on Long-Running Experiments</a> <a rel=\"mw:ExtLink nofollow\" href=\"https://web.archive.org/web/20160205202510/http://improbable.com/airchives/paperair/volume7/v7i3/long-run-7-3.html\" about=\"#mwt19\" typeof=\"mw:Transclusion\" id=\"mwow\" data-mw=\"{&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;template&quot;:{&quot;target&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;Webarchive&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:&quot;./Template:Webarchive&quot;},&quot;params&quot;:{&quot;url&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;https://web.archive.org/web/20160205202510/http://improbable.com/airchives/paperair/volume7/v7i3/long-run-7-3.html&quot;},&quot;date&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;5 February 2016&quot;}},&quot;i&quot;:0}}]}\">Archived</a><span about=\"#mwt19\" id=\"mwpA\"> 5 February 2016 at the </span><a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wayback_Machine\" title=\"Wayback Machine\" about=\"#mwt19\" id=\"mwpQ\">Wayback Machine</a>, <i id=\"mwpw\"><a rel=\"mw:ExtLink nofollow\" href=\"http://improbable.com/\" id=\"mwqA\">Improbable Research</a></i>.</span></li>\n<li about=\"#cite_note-7\" id=\"cite_note-7\" data-mw-footnote-number=\"7\"><span id=\"mwqQ\"><a href=\"#cite_ref-7\" rel=\"mw:referencedBy\" id=\"mwqg\"><span id=\"mwqw\">↑</span></a></span> <span id=\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-7\"><cite id=\"CITEREFOrd-Hume1977\" about=\"#mwt22\">Ord-Hume, Arthur W. J. G. (1977). <i id=\"mwrQ\">Perpetual Motion: The History of an Obsession</i>. George Allen &amp; Unwin. p.<span typeof=\"mw:Entity\" id=\"mwrg\">&nbsp;</span>172.</cite><span title=\"ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Abook&amp;rft.genre=book&amp;rft.btitle=Perpetual+Motion%3A+The+History+of+an+Obsession&amp;rft.pages=172&amp;rft.pub=George+Allen+%26+Unwin&amp;rft.date=1977&amp;rft.aulast=Ord-Hume&amp;rft.aufirst=Arthur+W.+J.+G.&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3AOxford+Electric+Bell\" about=\"#mwt22\" id=\"mwrw\"></span></span></li>\n</ol></div>\n\n</section><section data-mw-section-id=\"5\" id=\"mwsA\" aria-labelledby=\"Further_reading\">\n<ul id=\"mwsQ\"><li id=\"mwsg\">Willem Hackmann, \"<a rel=\"mw:ExtLink nofollow\" href=\"http://ppp.unipv.it/Collana/Pages/Libri/Saggi/Nuova%20Voltiana3_PDF/cap5/5new.pdf\" id=\"mwsw\">The Enigma of Volta's 'Contact Tension' and the